# CSS rules

## Location

- All CSS files live in `__REDESIGN2/KDB3/css/`.
- Mirror the JS region structure where useful: a region folder may have its own subfolder under `css/` (e.g. `css/widgets/`, `css/filters/`).

## File naming

- CSS files describe a UI surface, not an action. Names are noun-only.
- Examples: `header.css`, `footer.css`, `popup.css`, `widget.css`, `dropdown.css`.
- One file per visible UI area, owned by that area's region.
- Forbidden: `helpers.css`, `common.css`, `misc.css`, `styles.css`, `main.css` — vague catch-alls (consistent with `structure.mdc § Forbidden folder names`).

## Class-name namespacing

- Every class introduced by this project is prefixed `kdb-` to avoid collision with Cognos host styles.
- Use kebab-case (per `naming.mdc § Identifier casing`).
- Examples: `.kdb-header`, `.kdb-widget-title`, `.kdb-popup-option`.
- Forbidden: unprefixed class names in our CSS (`.header`, `.title`) — these can collide with Cognos.

## Selector discipline

- Prefer class selectors. Avoid styling by tag (`div`, `span`) or by Cognos-internal class.
- Do not target Cognos host classes (`.wa-*`, `.dashboard-*`, etc.) from our CSS — those are not our contract. If styling against host structure is needed, scope strictly under a `.kdb-*` ancestor.
- Avoid `!important`. If unavoidable, comment why.

## Loading

- CSS files are loaded via the bootstrap (per the init orchestrator), not by individual modules.
- Do not inject `<style>` tags from feature code. Use a CSS file.
